.TH DATAPUMP-TUI 1 "September 2026" "Data Pump" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
datapump-tui \- Data Pump terminal interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B datapump-tui
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs the shared Data Pump application using ncurses on POSIX systems or the
native Windows console API on Windows.
It uses the same commands, settings, receive restrictions and modem behavior as
the other frontends. A display server is not required.
Use an interactive terminal, including an SSH session allocated with ssh -t.
Audio devices are those of the computer running Data Pump.
.SH KEYBOARD
Tab and Shift-Tab move focus, stopping at the first or last available control.
This also applies to the input and buttons in file dialogs.
Page-Up and Page-Down scroll about 10 percent of the visible content height,
with a minimum of one line. Arrow keys navigate choices and lists and edit
text. Home, End, Backspace and Delete retain their usual editing roles.
F1 shows help. F2 sends Ctrl-Enter and F3 sends Shift-Enter for terminals that
cannot distinguish these modified Enter keys. F4 opens choices and editable
presets when Alt-Up or Alt-Down is unavailable. Ctrl-Q closes the application.
Ctrl-Enter sends the focused Fast or Legacy draft through its shared send policy.
Distinct modified-Enter sequences in CSI-u and xterm modifyOtherKeys format are
recognized. Terminal configurations that send ordinary Return for Ctrl-Enter,
including the default Konsole key table and usual Linux console mappings,
require F2 instead.
For a custom Konsole keyboard profile, map
.B Return+Ctrl-Shift
to
.B \eE[13;5u
in Settings, Edit Current Profile, Keyboard. This mapping makes Ctrl-Enter
distinct while leaving plain Return unchanged; Data Pump does not modify
terminal profiles itself.
Editable choices accept typed values as well as selections from presets.
The shared presentation supplies the interaction hints for the focused control.
.PP
The POSIX host handles correctly framed bracketed paste as one text event, including
embedded newlines. Pastes exceeding 4 MiB are rejected completely, preserving
the draft and selection. The terminal protocol cannot authenticate an embedded
paste-ending sequence; shared received-text filtering removes its control bytes.
The Windows console host and terminals without bracketed paste cannot
reliably distinguish pasted keys from typed keys. Use a terminal with bracketed
paste for multiline or untrusted clipboard contents.
.SH MOUSE
Konsole and other terminals with mouse reporting support pointer selection and
scrolling. Local Linux virtual-console mouse support depends on ncurses and an
available GPM service. Keyboard operation does not require mouse support.
Hold the terminal emulator's selection modifier (usually Shift) to select text
for its clipboard. Data Pump does not send OSC52 clipboard sequences or launch
folder openers from a terminal session.
.SH DISPLAY
Waterfall and constellation plots use colored ASCII intensity characters when
the terminal supports color, with a grayscale fallback.
QR images use fixed half-block glyphs on capable terminals, or two ASCII columns
per module, and preserve the quiet zone. Enlarge the plot when prompted; a QR
code cannot be reduced below one sample per module. QR terminal rendering uses
full contrast while retaining the explicit off setting.
Unsupported display bytes become underscores; local UTF-8 editor data is kept
independently of the terminal's display capability. Received content retains the
shared ASCII restriction before reaching the terminal sink. The renderer's fixed
block glyphs do not permit untrusted Unicode or terminal-control bytes.
.PP
Resize the terminal to change the layout. Large terminals are bounded to a
400-column by 160-row application surface. POSIX ncurses output is buffered with a fixed
upper bound and drained without blocking receiver polling. On a slow connection
newer presentation waits for earlier terminal output while application polling
continues. The Windows host writes the bounded surface using the native console
API; blocked-reader behavior through ConPTY remains unqualified.
.SH OPTIONS
Use
.B --help
for shared launch options,
.B --simulation
for simulated audio, and
.B --monochrome
for an uncolored display.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR datapump-gui (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ncurses (3)
